spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md:135:## T10 — Cross-tab outbox ownership and leases
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md-136-Reasoning: Multi-tab exactly-once (R1-q16 B) is the riskiest 
client protocol; proven right after fenced recovery, before unrelated features.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md-137-Task: All tabs of the same user share the outbox and show each 
item once; per conversation only the oldest unresolved item is eligible and one tab holds an atomic drain claim 
(lease: request timeout 30 s, lease 45 s) that ends on tab close/crash or expiry, after which another tab fences and 
resumes; when connectivity returns, an attempt started before or during the outage is superseded immediately under a 
new generation without waiting for the timeout or lease; no two create requests of one conversation overlap across 
tabs; cancel is atomic against claims and propagates to every tab.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md-138-Done when: AC-005, AC-006, AC-041, and the AC-045 E2E cases 
hold with two real tabs, including an attempt started during an active outage delivered within 10 s of recovery.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md-139-Verify by: New E2E specs (two tabs, held create, tab close, 
hung lease, attempt dispatched during outage, simultaneous online, cancel/claim race, network-log overlap check) pass 
3 consecutive runs in Chrome and Edge; regression suites pass.
